1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,200
This may sound strange, but I'm a big fan of the concrete block.

2
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,880
The first concrete blocks were manufactured in 1868.

3
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:25,640
It was a very simple idea.

4
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:34,880
Concrete blocks became the most used construction unit in the world.

5
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:39,480
They enabled us to build things that were larger than us, buildings, bridges, one brick

6
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:41,080
at a time.

7
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:46,080
Essentially, concrete blocks had become the building block of our time.

8
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:50,600
Almost 100 years later, in 1947, Lego came out with this.

9
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:53,280
It was called the automatic binding brick.

10
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:57,600
In a few short years, Lego bricks took place in every household.

11
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,920
It's estimated that over 400 billion bricks have been produced, or 75 bricks for every

12
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:04,200
person on the planet.

13
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:08,240
You didn't have to be an engineer to make beautiful houses, beautiful bridges, beautiful

14
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:09,240
buildings.

15
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:10,240
Lego made it accessible.

16
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:14,640
Lego had essentially taken the concrete block, the building block of the world, and made

17
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:17,600
it into the building block of our imagination.

18
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:22,920
Meanwhile, the exact same year, at Bell Labs, the next revolution was about to be announced.

19
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:24,480
The next building block.

20
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:29,480
The transistor was a small plastic unit that would take us from a world of static bricks

21
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:33,960
piled on top of each other to a world where everything was interactive.

22
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:39,200
Like the concrete block, the transistor allows you to build much larger, more complex circuits,

23
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:42,160
one brick at a time, but there's a main difference.

24
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:44,440
The transistor was only for experts.

25
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:49,680
I personally don't accept this, that the building block of our time is reserved for experts,

26
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,960
so I decided to change that.

27
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:56,900
Eight years ago, when I was at the Media Lab, I started exploring this idea of how to put

28
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:01,200
the power of engineers in the hands of artists and designers.

29
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:03,760
A few years ago, I started developing little bits.

30
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:08,200
Let me show you how they work.

31
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:14,060
Little bits are electronic modules with each one specific function.

32
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:20,960
They're pre-engineered to be light, sound, motors, and sensors.

33
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:26,620
And the best part about it is they snap together with magnets.

34
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:29,680
So you can't put them the wrong way.

35
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:31,460
The bricks are color coded.

36
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:36,220
Green is output, blue is power, pink is input, and orange is wire.

37
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:40,860
So all you need to do is snap a blue to a green, and very quickly, you can start making

38
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:42,620
larger circuits.

39
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:45,900
You put a blue to a green, you can make light.

40
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:50,540
You can put a knob in between.

41
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:54,140
And now you've made a little dimmer.

42
00:02:54,140 --> 00:03:01,980
Switch out the dimmer, the knob, for a pulse module, which is here.

43
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:05,300
And now you've made a little blinker.

44
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:11,900
Add this buzzer for some extra punch, and you've created a noise machine.

45
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:13,220
I'm going to stop then.

46
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:18,380
So beyond simple play, little bits are actually pretty powerful.

47
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:22,900
Instead of having to program, to wire, to solder, little bits allow you to program using

48
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:24,940
very simple intuitive gestures.

49
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:29,160
So to make this blink faster or slower, you would just turn this knob and basically make

50
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:32,180
it pulse faster or slower.

51
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:35,800
The idea behind little bits is that it's a growing library.

52
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:39,860
We want to make every single interaction in the world into a ready-to-use brick.

53
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:44,340
Lights, sounds, solar panels, motors, everything should be accessible.

54
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:47,820
We've been giving little bits to kids and seeing them play with them, and it's been

55
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:49,140
an incredible experience.

56
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:53,260
The nicest thing is how they start to understand the electronics around them from every day

57
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:54,840
that they don't learn at schools.

58
00:03:54,840 --> 00:04:00,420
For example, how a nightlight works, or why an elevator door stays open, or how an iPod

59
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:02,540
responds to touch.

60
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:04,780
We've also been taking little bits to design schools.

61
00:04:04,780 --> 00:04:09,020
So for example, we've had designers with no experience with electronics whatsoever start

62
00:04:09,060 --> 00:04:11,740
to play with little bits as a material.

63
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:15,740
Here you see, with felt and paper water bottles, we have Jordi making ...

64
00:04:15,740 --> 00:04:26,260
A few weeks ago, we took little bits to RISD and gave them to some designers with no experience

65
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:32,140
in engineering whatsoever, just cardboard, wood, and paper, and told them, make something.

66
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:38,260
Here's an example of a project they made, a motion-activated confetti cannonball.

67
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:41,740
Oh, wait, this is actually my favorite project.

68
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:53,020
It's a lobster made of Play-Doh that's afraid of the dark.

69
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:56,620
To these non-engineers, little bits became another material.

70
00:04:56,620 --> 00:05:00,140
Electronics became just another material, and we want to make this material accessible

71
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:01,140
to everyone.

72
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:02,560
So little bits is open source.

73
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:06,180
You can go on the website, download all the design files, make them yourself.

74
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:11,940
We want to encourage a world of creators, of inventors, of contributors, because this

75
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:14,840
world that we live in, this interactive world, is ours.

76
00:05:14,840 --> 00:05:17,340
So go ahead and start inventing.

77
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:17,700
Thank you.

